  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Sunday)\nIssued at 1110 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCold Canadian high pressure will settle down into the north-central \npart of the country over the next couple days. This will act to \nreinforce the colder air that has moved into the region today behind \nthe front. Daytime highs will trend downward through Tuesday, \ndropping 10 to 15 degrees below average for this time of year. \nWidespread freezing temps are expected for overnight lows this week, \nwith some areas seeing hard or killing freezes.\n\nAn upper wave coming in from the Pacific on Monday will drop \nsoutheast from the central Rockies down into Texas on Tuesday.\nIncreasing clouds over the region with the wave Monday night may \nkeep overnight lows from dropping as cold as the model blend (NBM) \nwould suggest. Leaned closer to MOS instead. Models have \nconsistently shown that the best forcing with this wave will track \nsouth of us from Texas into southern AR and LA Tuesday night.\nAvailable moisture will be lacking as well, so this wave is expected \nto pass across the south-central CONUS without measurable precip \nexpected for our area.\n\nSoutherly winds will return during the latter part of the week, with \na warming trend expected through the Thanksgiving holiday weekend \nperiod. Quiet weather is expected to continue as well and should not \nimpact holiday plans. A storm tracking up the eastern seaboard, and \nstorminess in the Pacific Northwest may impact travelers headed \nin these directions.\n\nLacy",
